CAT = "ea2Entities"; SEG = "ENT"
# Business entities and considerations: entity types, classification, EIN, tax years, reporting, hobbies.
Q = [
("An LLC owned by one individual files no Form 8832. For federal income tax it is:",
 "Disregarded from its owner", ["Taxed as a partnership", "Taxed as a C corporation", "Taxed as an S corporation"],
 "Under Reg. 301.7701-3(b)(1), a domestic eligible entity with a single owner is disregarded by default, so an individual owner reports the business on Schedule C. A partnership needs at least two members."),
("A domestic LLC with three members never files Form 8832. Its default classification is:",
 "Partnership", ["Disregarded entity", "C corporation", "S corporation"],
 "Reg. 301.7701-3(b)(1) classifies a domestic eligible entity with two or more members as a partnership unless it elects otherwise. S status needs a corporation, or an entity electing to be one, plus Form 2553."),
("The effective date chosen on Form 8832 can be no earlier than how many days before the election is filed?",
 "75 days", ["60 days", "90 days", "30 days"],
 "Reg. 301.7701-3(c)(1)(iii) lets an entity classification election take effect no more than 75 days before, and no more than 12 months after, the date Form 8832 is filed. Rev. Proc. 2009-41 offers late election relief."),
("Spouses who own an unincorporated business together, file jointly and elect qualified joint venture status report its income:",
 "On a Schedule C for each", ["On one Form 1065", "On one spouse's Schedule C", "On one Schedule C, split"],
 "A qualified joint venture election under section 761(f) lets each spouse report a share of income and expense on a separate Schedule C and Schedule SE instead of filing a partnership return."),
("The qualified joint venture election is not available when the spouses run the business through:",
 "A state-law LLC", ["Joint ownership as spouses", "A business with employees", "A business that has a loss"],
 "A qualified joint venture covers a business the spouses own directly. A business held in a state-law entity such as an LLC files as a partnership, or the couple may in a community property state treat it under Rev. Proc. 2002-69."),
("Which sole proprietor must get an employer identification number?",
 "One who hires an employee", ["One who files a Schedule C", "One with over $400 of profit", "One who works from home"],
 "A sole proprietor needs an EIN to pay wages, file excise or pension plan returns, or in a few other cases. A proprietor with no employees may use a Social Security number on Schedule C and Schedule SE."),
("A sole proprietor takes in a partner and the business now operates as a partnership. Its EIN:",
 "Must be a new number", ["Can stay the same", "Passes to the new partner", "Is no longer required"],
 "A partnership is a new taxpayer, so it needs its own EIN, applied for on Form SS-4 or online. The proprietor's old number belonged to the proprietorship."),
("A newly formed C corporation that is not a personal service corporation may adopt for its first return:",
 "Any tax year it chooses", ["Only a calendar year", "Its majority owner's year", "Only a June 30 year"],
 "A new C corporation adopts its tax year by filing its first return on that basis, and may pick a calendar or any fiscal year. Partnerships, S corporations and personal service corporations face required-year rules instead."),
("When partners holding a majority interest share the same tax year, the partnership must generally use:",
 "That majority-interest year", ["A calendar year in all cases", "The least aggregate deferral year", "Any year the partners agree to"],
 "Section 706(b) applies the majority-interest year first, then the year of all principal partners, then the year giving the least aggregate deferral. A business purpose or a section 444 election can allow another year."),
("A partnership wants a fiscal year other than its required year without showing a business purpose. It may make a:",
 "Section 444 election", ["Section 754 election", "Section 1362 election", "Section 83(b) election"],
 "Section 444, made on Form 8716, lets a partnership, S corporation or personal service corporation keep a limited deferral period, with required payments under section 7519 for partnerships and S corporations."),
("An employer must file Form W-2 Copy A for a year's wages with the Social Security Administration by:",
 "January 31 of the next year", ["February 28 of the next year", "March 31 of the next year", "February 15 of the next year"],
 "Copy A of Form W-2 and Form W-3 are due to the SSA by January 31 after the year ends, the same day employees must receive their copies, moving to the next business day when January 31 falls on a weekend or holiday. The older paper and electronic deadlines in late February and March no longer apply to W-2s."),
("A new employee never gives the employer a Form W-4. The employer withholds income tax as if the employee were:",
 "Single, no other entries", ["Married filing jointly", "Head of household", "Exempt until it arrives"],
 "Publication 15 tells employers to withhold as for a single or married filing separately employee with no other entries in Steps 2, 3 and 4 when no valid Form W-4 is furnished."),
("A business paid an unincorporated contractor $5,000 for repair services in 2025. It reports the payment on:",
 "Form 1099-NEC", ["Form 1099-MISC", "Form W-2", "Form 1099-K"],
 "Payments of $600 or more for services by a nonemployee are nonemployee compensation on Form 1099-NEC. Form 1099-MISC still covers items such as rents and royalties, and Form 1099-K is filed by payment settlement entities."),
("An activity other than horse breeding is presumed to be engaged in for profit if it shows a profit in:",
 "3 of the last 5 years", ["2 of the last 5 years", "3 of the last 7 years", "1 of the last 3 years"],
 "Section 183(d) presumes a profit motive if gross income exceeds deductions in 3 of 5 consecutive years. The 2 of 7 years test applies only to breeding, training, showing or racing horses."),
("A taxpayer who wants to delay the IRS's decision on whether a new activity is a hobby files:",
 "Form 5213", ["Form 8832", "Form 1128", "Form 3115"],
 "Form 5213 elects to postpone the profit-motive determination until the end of the fifth year (seventh for horses), and it extends the assessment period for that activity. Forms 8832, 1128 and 3115 deal with classification, tax years and methods."),
("A homeowners association that elects to be taxed under section 528 files:",
 "Form 1120-H", ["Form 990-T", "Form 1120", "Form 990-EZ"],
 "A qualifying homeowners association may elect each year on Form 1120-H to exclude exempt function income such as member dues and pay tax only on other net income. Without the election it files Form 1120."),
]
